//? Title: Rate-monotonic scheduling: actual periodic jobs, priorities and deadline evidence
//? Subtitle: Actual canonical histories, retained original fixtures and explicit controls
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Periodic real-time tasks must complete every released job before its deadline.
Rate-monotonic scheduling assigns higher fixed priority to shorter periods,
while a utilization bound gives a sufficient schedulability screen. This report
uses the already checked native job/tick producer and timeline plot, retains the
original three tasks and heavier control, and distinguishes a failed sufficient
test from an actual deadline miss.
)

\toc

> Mechanism and mathematical scope

Assume a preemptive single processor, independent synchronous periodic tasks,
execution cost C_i and relative deadline equal to period T_i. Rate-monotonic
priorities order increasing T_i, with the native documented deterministic tie
rules.
$$ U=\sum_i C_i/T_i,\qquad U\le n(2^{1/n}-1)\ \Longrightarrow\ \text{RMS schedulable}. $$
\alttext(The Liu-Layland utilization bound is sufficient under the implicit-deadline periodic model.)
The bound follows from the worst-case fixed-priority task sets, not from a
statement that higher-utilization sets always fail. Harmonic periods can
schedule at utilization1. The simulator instead follows actual releases,
remaining work, selected jobs and deadline boundaries. A job unfinished at its
deadline is a miss even if it later completes; unfinished jobs are not dropped
when the next period releases. Simulation over a finite horizon checks only
deadlines through that horizon. It is evidence for that run, not a general proof
for arbitrary offsets/deadlines/resource blocking.

> A tiny actual producing history

The table and native concept diagram read the same producing execution. Every
block below is a complete editable C++ program.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/rate_monotonic_tiny.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <datamunge/plot/plot.hpp>
#include <datamunge/plot/diagram.hpp>
#include <datamunge/algorithms/modular_exponentiation.hpp>
#include <datamunge/algorithms/integer_arithmetic.hpp>
#include <filesystem>
#include <print>
#include <algorithm>
#include <cmath>
#include <limits>
#include <climits>
#include <datamunge/algorithms/number_theory.hpp>
#include <numeric>
#include <numbers>
#include <random>
#include <string>
#include <vector>
#include <map>
#include <set>
#include <array>
#include <tuple>
#include <datamunge/algorithms/prufer.hpp>
#include <datamunge/algorithms/flow_diagram.hpp>
#include <datamunge/geometry/hull_algorithms.hpp>
#include <datamunge/random/sampling_analysis.hpp>
#include <iomanip>
#include <datamunge/algorithms/qr_algorithm.hpp>
#include <datamunge/stats/lm.hpp>
#include <datamunge/algorithms/arithmetic_coding.hpp>
#include <datamunge/nn/rbf.hpp>
#include <datamunge/nn/model_plot.hpp>
#include <bit>
#include <datamunge/algorithms/realtime_scheduling.hpp>
int main() {
    namespace a = dm::algorithms;
    namespace g = dm::graphics;
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");

    const std::vector<a::RTTask> tasks{{7, 2, 1}, {99, 4, 1}};
    auto t = a::rt_trace(tasks, 4, a::RTPolicy::rate_monotonic);
    std::print("\n| actual job id | task id | release | deadline | executed | completion | missed |\n| ---: " "| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | --- |\n");
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < t.jobs.size(); ++i) {
        const auto &j = t.jobs[i];
        std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n", i,
                   t.tasks[j.task_index].id, j.release, j.deadline, j.executed,
                   j.completion, j.missed);
    }

    g::Diagram d;
    d.node("n0", {0, 0},
           std::format("Actual priority ids\n{}",
                       a::rate_monotonic_priorities(tasks)));
    d.node("n1", {10, 0},
           std::format("Actual first tick job\n{}", t.tick_jobs[0]));
    d.node("n2", {20, 0},
           std::format("Actual completed jobs / misses\n{} / {}",
                       t.work.jobs_completed, t.work.deadline_misses));
    d.edge("n0", "n1").edge("n1", "n2");
    d.save_svg("build/rate_monotonic_tiny.svg");
}
```
