> API and interpretation

The original heavier task set is shown with its actual RMS and EDF outcomes
instead of asserting failure from a failed bound screen. Sparse external task
ids are labels, not array row positions. The existing canonical API validates
every task, preserves overdue backlog and checks the boundary H. Its native plot
maps task ids to input-order rows. This report reuses that producer rather than
creating another scheduler or timeline renderer. Counters are selected
simulation events, not response-time analysis or wall-clock overhead.

Reusable producers, fixtures and histories live in the canonical C++ library.
Captured records own their values and do not come from a separate report replay.
Selected counters and record/scalar policies are specified at their API
boundaries; they are not peak-byte or wall-time budgets. Synthetic structural
inputs are controlled fixtures for these mechanisms.

> References

- [Liu and Layland, Scheduling Algorithms for Multiprogramming in a Hard-Real-Time Environment (1973)|https://doi.org/10.1145/321738.321743]

